welcomeName(marc);˜You are *so* close to getting this to work. Your function definition is actually 100% correct! 

The error happens on the very last line when you try to **call** the function: `welcomeName(marc);`

### The Problem:
Because `marc` does not have quotes around it on that last line, JavaScript thinks you are trying to pass a **variable** named `marc`. Since you haven't created a variable called `marc`, the computer panics and throws a `ReferenceError: marc is not defined`.

### The Fix:
You need to put quotes around `'marc'` when you call the function so JavaScript knows it is a literal **String** (text), not a variable.

```javascript
function welcomeName (name) {
  if (name === 'marc'){
    console.log('Hello, ' + name);
  }
}

// FIX: Added quotes around 'marc'
welcomeName('marc'); 
```

### One quick thing to notice:
Right now, your function has an `if` statement that checks if the name is strictly equal to `'marc'`. This means:
* `welcomeName('marc');` will print `"Hello, marc"`
* `welcomeName('kana');` will print **nothing at all**, because Kana isn't Marc!

If you want the function to welcome *anybody* who passes their name in, you can remove the `if` statement entirely:

```javascript
function welcomeName (name) {
  // This will now work for any name you type in!
  console.log('Hello, ' + name); 
}

welcomeName('marc'); // Prints: Hello, marc
welcomeName('kana'); // Prints: Hello, kana
```

You are doing great. Functions can be tricky to wrap your head around at first, but you've pretty much got it. 

