## Phase 3: DOM Manipulation (Making Web Pages Interactive)

This is the exact moment where JavaScript stops being just math and logic in a blank console, and starts becoming **alive** on a website.

The **DOM** (Document Object Model) is how JavaScript looks at an HTML page. To JavaScript, every HTML tag (`<h1>`, `<button>`, `<p>`) is an object that it can select, change, or destroy.



### Step 1: Selecting an HTML element
Before you can change an element, you have to tell JavaScript to go find it. We use `document.querySelector()` to grab elements using their CSS selectors.

```html
<h1 id="main-title">Hello World</h1>
<button class="click-btn">Click Me</button>
```

```javascript
// This is your JavaScript selecting those elements
let title = document.querySelector('#main-title');
let button = document.querySelector('.click-btn');
```

### Step 2: Changing the element
Once you have selected it, you can change its text using `.textContent` or change its style!

```javascript
title.textContent = "Welcome to my JS site!";
title.style.color = "red"; // Changes the text color to red!
```

---

### üõ†Ô∏è Challenge 9:
