```

1. Use `document.querySelector()` with the correct symbol to select that class and save it in a variable called `status`.
2. Change its `.textContent` to `"Logged In"`.√
4why do we use document.querySelector is it built in äYes, absolutely! **`document.querySelector()`** is completely built-in. You don't need to install anything or download any extra files to use it. 

Every web browser (like Chrome, Safari, Firefox, or Edge) has a built-in JavaScript engine. When the browser loads an HTML page, it automatically creates a giant, invisible JavaScript object called **`document`**. 

The `document` object represents your entire web page, and `querySelector()` is a built-in function (a method) attached to it that allows you to search through that page.

---

### Why do we use it? (The Big Advantage)

Before `document.querySelector()` came along, developers had to use different, clunky commands depending on what they were looking for:
* To find an ID: `document.getElementById('header')`
* To find a Class: `document.getElementsByClassName('btn')`
* To find a Tag: `document.getElementsByTagName('p')`

`document.querySelector()` was invented to replace all of them with **one single superpower**. It lets you use the exact same selectors you already use in **CSS**. 



* Want a tag? `document.querySelector('h1')`
* Want a class? `document.querySelector('.my-class')`
* Want an ID? `document.querySelector('#my-id')`
* Want something complex? `document.querySelector('div ul li.active')`

It keeps your code incredibly clean because you only have to memorize one tool to grab anything you want on the screen.

---

### Ready to try it out?

Let's look back at our paragraph example:
```html
<p class="status-text">Logged Out</p>
```

How would you use this built-in `document.querySelector()` tool to grab this paragraph by its class and change its text to `"Logged In"`? Give it a shot!Å
°<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <title> This is fun </title>
    <h1 id="main-title"> Hello world</h1>
    <button class = "Click-button"> click me </button>
    <script>
        let title = Document.querySelector('#main-title');
        let button= Document.querySelector('.click-btn');
        title.textContent="This is going to be fun";
        title.style.color='red';
    </script>
</html> what did i mess up in here⁄You are getting incredibly close to writing full, working web pages! You set up the HTML structure and paired it with the JavaScript beautifully. 

However, your code has **three small bugs** that will prevent it from working in a real browser. Two are JavaScript typos, and one is an HTML/CSS naming mismatch.

Here is exactly what went wrong and how to fix it:

---

### 1. Capitalization Typo (`Document` vs `document`)
JavaScript is strictly case-sensitive. The built-in object is lowercase **`document`**. Because you wrote it with a capital **`D`** (`Document.querySelector`), JavaScript will break and say *`Document is not defined`*.

### 2. The Naming Mismatch (`Click-button` vs `click-btn`)
Look at how you named your button in the HTML versus how you looked for it in the JavaScript:
* **HTML:** `class="Click-button"` (Capital C, spelled out "button")
* **JavaScript:** `querySelector('.click-btn')` (Lowercase c, shortened to "btn")

JavaScript won't know those are supposed to be the same thing. They need to match exactly.

### 3. Missing `<body>` Tag (HTML Best Practice)
While browsers are forgiving, visible elements like `<h1>` and `<button>` should always live inside a `<body>` tag, and your `<script>` tag should sit right at the bottom of the body.

---

### The Fixed, Working Code

Here is your exact code with those three spots cleaned up. If you save this as an `.html` file and open it in a browser, it will work perfectly!

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>This is fun</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1 id="main-title">Hello world</h1>
    <button class="click-btn">click me</button>

    <script>
        // FIX: Changed 'Document' to lowercase 'document'
        let title = document.querySelector('#main-title');
        let button = document.querySelector('.click-btn');
        
        title.textContent = "This is going to be fun";
        title.style.color = 'red';
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### The Next Step: Events
Notice how the moment the page loads, the text instantly changes to red and says *"This is going to be fun"*? 

The next step in our roadmap is **Events**‚Äîmaking that change happen *only* when a user actually clicks the button! 

Are you ready to see how to wire up that click button?à
+ok lets make something like that out of it ÿLet's do it! This is where coding becomes incredibly satisfying. 

To make your web page wait for a user to click the button before changing the text, we use an **Event Listener**. 

Think of an Event Listener like a security guard waiting at a door. The guard does nothing until a specific "event" happens (like someone knocking). In JavaScript, we tell the button to listen for a `'click'` event, and when it happens, run a function!



---

### The Secret Ingredient: `.addEventListener()`

Here is how we write it:
```javascript
button.addEventListener('click', function() {
    // Put whatever code you want to run AFTER the click inside here!
    title.textContent = "This is going to be fun";
    title.style.color = 'red';
});
```

---

