        //    - Change body.style.color to "#ffffff" (White text)
        //    - Change the heading's textContent to "Dark Mode Active"
        
        //    Else:
        //    - Turn the background back to "#ffffff"
        //    - Turn the text color back to "#333333"
        //    - Change the heading's textContent back to "Light Mode Active"

    </script>
</body>
</html>âYou targeted the correct variables and set up your logic beautifully, but there are **two critical syntax errors** inside your `if` statement line that will break the code. 

Here are the exact points of error:

---

### 1. Missing Quotes Around the RGB Value
In JavaScript, CSS values like `rgb(255, 255, 255)` or hex codes are strings. Because there are no quotation marks around `rgb(255,255,255)`, JavaScript thinks you are trying to call a nonexistent function named `rgb()` and will throw a *ReferenceError*. 

### 2. Spacing Inside the RGB String
When JavaScript reads an inline style color from the DOM, it formats the string very strictly with spaces after the commas: `"rgb(255, 255, 255)"`. If your string is missing those spaces (`"rgb(255,255,255)"`), the comparison will fail, and the `else` block will run instead.

---

Fix that comparison line, and your theme switcher will run perfectly!ü
