what do you mean by elements ÆWhen we talk about **elements** in web development, we are talking about the individual building blocks that make up a webpage. 

Think of an HTML file as a recipe, and **elements** as the actual ingredients you put into it. A button, a heading, a paragraph, or an image are all individual elements.

---

### The Anatomy of an Element

An element is usually made up of three parts: an **opening tag**, the **content**, and a **closing tag**.



Here is a breakdown using a standard paragraph element:
* **`</p>` (Closing Tag):** Marks the end of the element. (Notice the forward slash `/`).
* **`Hello World` (Content):** The actual text or data that shows up on the screen.
* **`<p>` (Opening Tag):** Tells the browser where the element starts and what kind of element it is (`p` stands for paragraph).

---

### In JavaScript: Elements become "Objects"

When you use `document.querySelector('#bill')`, JavaScript goes into your HTML, looks for that specific element tag, and grabs it. 

Once JavaScript has a hold of that element, it treats it like a programmable object with **properties** you can read or change. Some of the most common properties attached to an element object are:

* **`.value`** : What a user typed inside the element (if it's an input field).
* **`.textContent`** : The text sitting inside the element (between the tags).
* **`.style`** : The visual design of the element (like background color or font size).

So when your code had `bill.input`, JavaScript got confused because `input` isn't a property that belongs to an element object‚Äîbut `.value` is!ñ
