Çso just instead of a simple space to gather the list we are create a more organised box where we can do a lot with the text right „**Spot on!** You hit the nail right on the head. 

By wrapping the text in a `<span>`, you are upgrading it from raw, loose text into a distinct, organized container. Once it is inside that container, you can manipulate it, style it, move it around, or even add features to it later (like making it fade out or getting a line struck through it when completed) without breaking the rest of the row.

Now you have the entire mental map of the system:
1. The **`input`** gathers the message.
2. The **`span`** protects and structures the text.
3. The **`button`** handles the deletion logic.
4. The **`li`** acts as the conveyor belt holding the span and button together.
5. The **`ul`** acts as the final destination on the webpage.

You have all the knowledge you need. Show me how you assemble these pieces inside the `<script>` tag of your Mission Control Todo List!◊,
