The CSS style rule for the list item container is set up like this:
```css
li {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
}
```
This tells the browser to push exactly **two** main child elements inside the `<li>` to opposite walls (one far left, one far right). 

By wrapping *both* of your `<span>` elements inside a single `infoDiv` and then putting that `infoDiv` into the `li`, you accidentally bundled them together into a single item. The browser sees your `infoDiv` as just **one** element, so it stays stuck on the left side with the player name and score smushed right next to each other.

### The Fix

You don't need a wrapper `infoDiv` for this layout. If you append the name `span` and the score `sspan` directly to the `li`, the CSS `justify-content: space-between` will work exactly as intended, instantly throwing the player name to the far left and the score to the far right.

To make the score look exactly like the design, you should also add the CSS style class to your score span:
```javascript
sspan.classList.add("score-val");
```Ó
FHow would it be saved in the local storage when many inputs are given £When a user submits multiple players, you hit a massive roadblock if you only use single keys like `"latestName"`. 

Every time you call `localStorage.setItem("latestName", nameInput.value);`, JavaScript **overwrites** whatever was previously there. You will only ever remember the absolute last player who registered.

To save an entire roster of multiple players simultaneously, you have to use a **JavaScript Array of Objects** combined with text conversion methods.

---

### üì¶ The Core Secret: JSON Stringification

`localStorage` is primitive; it only accepts strings of text. It cannot hold arrays (`[]`) or objects (`{}`). 

To bypass this restriction, we bundle all player data into objects, push them into a single array, and use a method called `JSON.stringify()` to smash that entire data structure into a single, massive text string.



---

### üïπÔ∏è How it Looks in Action (The Pattern)

Instead of saving strings one by one, your data structure will evolve to look like this in your computer's memory:

```javascript
// A master database array containing object records
let leaderboardData = [
    { name: "ACE", score: 9500 },
    { name: "SKY", score: 8200 },
    { name: "FOX", score: 7100 }
];
```

#### Step 1: Saving the Array
When a user adds a new player, you push a new object into your array, and then update the storage key by converting the whole collection into text:

```javascript
// Turn the array into a clean text string and lock it in storage
localStorage.setItem("gameLeaderboard", JSON.stringify(leaderboardData));
```
Inside the browser's storage locker, it saves it looking like this flat text:
`"[{"name":"ACE","score":9500},{"name":"SKY","score":8200}]"`

#### Step 2: Retrieving the Array on Load
When the page refreshes, you pull that text string out of the vault and use `JSON.parse()` to rebuild it back into a fully functional JavaScript array:

```javascript
let savedText = localStorage.getItem("gameLeaderboard");

// Convert the text string back into a real working array
let leaderboardData = JSON.parse(savedText); 
```

---

