Ai feel great to have learnt it what do you suggest me doing next ÀIt is an incredible feeling when code finally clicks, isn't it? You went from manually manipulating basic HTML text to building a fully functional, persistent data loop that interacts with the browser's internal engine. That is a massive milestone!

Now that you have conquered **Level 1 (Core DOM & Logic)** and unlocked the gateway to **Level 3 (Permanent Storage)**, your foundational muscles are solid. 

To take your engineering skills to the absolute next level, I suggest we tackle **The Array Evolution (Level 2)**. 

---

### üöÄ Why Arrays are Your Next Power-Up

Right now, your arcade leaderboard can only hold **one single score**. If player "ACE" registers, and then player "SKY" registers, ACE is wiped out of existence. 

To make this a real, professional app, we need to allow **infinite players** to join the leaderboard, and we want to **rank them from highest score to lowest score automatically**. 

To do that, we have to stop saving raw text strings directly to individual keys, and instead manage a **Roster Array of Objects**. 

---

### üèÜ Your Next Project: The Infinite Retro Roster

We are going to upgrade your Arcade Leaderboard. Instead of using `latestName`, your JavaScript will manage a master list that looks like this behind the scenes:

```javascript
let highScores = [
    { name: "ACE", score: 9900 },
    { name: "SKY", score: 8500 },
    { name: "FOX", score: 7200 }
];
```



This project will teach you three legendary developer skills:
1. **`Array.push()`**: How to dynamically add new data objects to an existing collection.
2. **`JSON.stringify` & `JSON.parse`**: How to compress an entire array into a single string so `localStorage` can save the whole roster at once.
3. **`Array.sort()`**: How to make JavaScript automatically calculate who is in 1st place, 2nd place, and 3rd place based on their scores.

