*how to say when are you coming in japaneseªIn Japanese, how you say **"When are you coming?"** depends entirely on who you are talking to (your friend, a coworker, or someone you need to be polite to). 

Here are the three most common ways to say it, broken down by politeness level:

### 1. Casual (For Friends, Family, or Peers)
Use this when talking to someone close to you. 

* **Japanese:** „ÅÑ„Å§Êù•„Çã„ÅÆÔºü
* **Pronunciation (Romaji):** *Itsu kuru no?*

**Breakdown:**
* **„ÅÑ„Å§ (*itsu*):** When
* **Êù•„Çã (*kuru*):** To come (casual/dictionary form)
* **„ÅÆ (*no*):** A casual question particle added to the end to make it sound friendly.

---

### 2. Polite (Standard Everyday Japanese)
This is the safest version to use. Use this with acquaintances, strangers, or coworkers you aren't close with.

* **Japanese:** „ÅÑ„Å§Êù•„Åæ„Åô„ÅãÔºü
* **Pronunciation (Romaji):** *Itsu kimasu ka?*

**Breakdown:**
* **„ÅÑ„Å§ (*itsu*):** When
* **Êù•„Åæ„Åô (*kimasu*):** To come (polite *-masu* form)
* **„Åã (*ka*):** The standard question mark particle.

---

### 3. Honorific / Formal (For Bosses, Seniors, or Customers)
Use this if you want to show a high level of respect to someone senior to you.

* **Japanese:** „ÅÑ„Å§„ÅÑ„Çâ„Å£„Åó„ÇÉ„ÅÑ„Åæ„Åô„ÅãÔºü
* **Pronunciation (Romaji):** *Itsu irasshaimasu ka?*

**Breakdown:**
* **„ÅÑ„Çâ„Å£„Åó„ÇÉ„ÅÑ„Åæ„Åô (*irasshaimasu*):** The respectful/honorific (*keigo*) version of the verb "to come."

---

### üí° Quick Vocabulary Tip
If you want to say **"When are you coming *here*?"**, just add the word for here (**„Ç≥„Ç≥ / *koko***) and the direction particle (**„Å´ / *ni***) to the very front of any of those sentences:

* *Casual:* **Koko ni** itsu kuru no? (Here when coming?)
* *Polite:* **Koko ni** itsu kimasu ka?“
;Yume o mirunova yamete kudasai¬†
Dame dayo what it means ? íYou are looking at a classic dramatic anime exchange! These two sentences are packed with emotion. If you translate them directly, they form a bittersweet conversation between someone telling another person to face reality, and the other refusing to give up.

Here is exactly what they mean and how the grammar breaks down:

---

### 1. Â§¢„ÇíË¶ã„Çã„ÅÆ„ÅØ„ÇÑ„ÇÅ„Å¶„Åè„Å†„Åï„ÅÑ 
*(Yume o miru no wa yamete kudasai)*

* **What it means:** **"Please stop dreaming"** or **"Please give up on your dreams."**

#### üõ†Ô∏è Word-by-Word Breakdown:
* **Â§¢ (*Yume*):** Dream
* **„ÇíË¶ã„Çã (*o miru*):** To see / To look at (In Japanese, you "see" a dream rather than "have" a dream).
* **„ÅÆ„ÅØ (*no wa*):** Converts "seeing a dream" into a noun phrase ("The act of dreaming").
* **„ÇÑ„ÇÅ„Å¶ (*yamete*):** The command form of *yameru* (to stop / quit).
* **„Åè„Å†„Åï„ÅÑ (*kudasai*):** Please.

---

### 2. „ÉÄ„É°„Å†„Çà
*(Dame dayo)*

* **What it means:** **"No way,"** **"It's no use,"** or **"I can't do that."**

#### üõ†Ô∏è Word-by-Word Breakdown:
* **„ÉÄ„É° (*Dame*):** No good / Impossible / Forbidden / Not allowed.
* **„Å†„Çà (*da yo*):** *Da* means "it is" (casual), and *yo* is an emphatic particle added to the end to add conviction or force to the statement (like saying, "I'm telling you, no!").

---

### üé≠ The Context: How They Fit Together

When put back-to-back, it reads like a dramatic movie script:

> **Person A:** "Please stop dreaming (wake up to reality)."
> **Person B:** "No way / I can't do that (I won't give up)."

It‚Äôs an excellent example of everyday conversational Japanese grammar! Are you trying to translate a specific scene or song you came across?ƒ
Iye maasen üôÑ∞Ah, you're continuing the anime dialogue! 

**"Iye maasen"** is a playful, phonetic spelling of:
**„Äå„ÅÑ„ÅÑ„Åà„ÄÅ„ÅÑ„Åæ„Åõ„Çì„Äç (Iie, imasen)** or **„Äå„ÅÑ„ÅÑ„Åà„ÄÅ„Åó„Åæ„Åõ„Çì„Äç (Iie, shimasen)** depending on the exact context, but it most likely means:

**"No, I won't"** or **"No, there isn't / I'm not here."**

If we look at it as a direct reply to the previous line (*"Please stop dreaming" -> "No way" -> "I won't!"*), it translates to:
* **"No, I won't (stop dreaming)!"** or **"No, absolutely not!"**

### üõ†Ô∏è Breaking down the actual Japanese words:
* **„ÅÑ„ÅÑ„Åà (*Iie*):** No.
* **„Äú„Åæ„Åõ„Çì (*-masen*):** The polite, negative ending for Japanese verbs (meaning "do not" or "will not"). 

The eye-roll emoji (üôÑ) fits it perfectly‚Äîit‚Äôs a sassy, stubborn refusal to back down or stop chasing that dream! 

