## üåé What Actually is the DOM?

When a browser loads a website, it reads your raw HTML file and turns it into a giant, live JavaScript **Object** called the **DOM** (Document Object Model). 

Think of your webpage like a tree where every single HTML tag is a branch or a leaf. In JavaScript, we call these branches and leaves **Elements** or **Nodes**. 



Because the DOM is just an object, JavaScript can reach into it, read the tags, change their styles, delete them, or add new ones on the fly. 

To do anything, your script first needs to say: *"Hey webpage, go find this specific HTML tag for me so I can mess with it."*

---

## üéØ Stage 1: Target and Capture (Selecting Elements)

We use the global `document` object to hunt for elements. There is one modern master method you need to know for this: **`document.querySelector()`**.

You pass it the exact same selectors you use in **CSS**:
* To grab a tag name: `document.querySelector("h1")`
* To grab an ID: `document.querySelector("#submit-btn")`
* To grab a Class: `document.querySelector(".error-message")`

```javascript
// Capturing the elements and storing them in variables
let mainHeading = document.querySelector("h1");
let loginButton = document.querySelector("#login");
```

---

## üõ†Ô∏è Stage 2: Modify the Target

Once you have captured an element inside a variable, you can manipulate its properties using dot-notation. The two most common properties you will modify are:

### 1. `.textContent` (Changing Text)
This reads or rewrites the text sitting inside the HTML tags.
```javascript
let heading = document.querySelector("h1");
heading.textContent = "Welcome to the Matrix"; // Changes the text on the screen instantly!
```

### 2. `.style` (Changing CSS Properties)
Every element has a `.style` object inside it. You can change any CSS property, but camelCase replaces hyphens (e.g., `background-color` becomes `backgroundColor`).
```javascript
let box = document.querySelector(".box");
box.style.backgroundColor = "blue";
box.style.borderRadius = "10px";
```

---

