Since you've locked down the fundamentals of data, logic, loops, functions, and standard interactive event listeners, it's time to learn how to deal with **Collections of Elements**. 

This brings back your loop skills to control multiple items at once.

---

## üë• The Loop + DOM Connection: Controlling Lists

Often, you won't just want to change *one* button or *one* paragraph. You will have a whole list of items‚Äîlike a list of items in a shopping cart, a list of notifications, or a group of user cards.

If you use `document.querySelectorAll(".card")`, JavaScript returns a **NodeList** (which acts almost exactly like an Array) containing all elements that match that class.

You cannot change them all at once by writing `cards.style.color = "red"`. Because it's a list, you have to use a `for` loop to step through each item one by one!

```javascript
let cards = document.querySelectorAll(".card");

for (let i = 0; i < cards.length; i++) {
    // Target the specific card at index 'i' and modify it!
    cards[i].style.border = "2px solid blue";
}
```

---

