You ran into a classic DOM trap that catches almost every developer. 

---

### üö® The Culprit Lines

Look closely at this sequence in your script:

```javascript
// 1. You built a beautiful HTML string with a button inside it
newElement.innerHTML = `<span>üìå ${inputValue}</span> <button class="delete-btn">Delete</button>`;

// 2. RIGHT HERE IS THE TRAP:
newElement.textContent = taskInput.value; 
```

### üí£ Why it broke:
The `.textContent` property updates the raw, unformatted text of an element. By running it *after* `.innerHTML`, you told JavaScript: *"Hey, ignore that whole HTML structure with the span and the delete button I just made, wipe it all out, and just make this a plain text string."*

Because the delete button was completely wiped out of existence by that line, your next line `newElement.querySelector(".delete-btn")` returned `null`, crashing the code!

---

### üõ†Ô∏è The Fix

All you have to do is **delete that `.textContent` line completely**. Your `.innerHTML` line is already doing all the work of inserting the `inputValue`!

Here is your exact `<script>` block, corrected and ready to run:

```javascript
<script>
    const taskInput = document.querySelector("#task-input");
    const addBtn = document.querySelector("#add-btn");
    const taskList = document.querySelector("#task-list");

    addBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const inputValue = taskInput.value;
        if (inputValue === "") return; // Guard clause

        // Create the row element
        const newElement = document.createElement("li");
        
        // Inject the text AND the button simultaneously!
        newElement.innerHTML = `<span>üìå ${inputValue}</span> <button class="delete-btn">Delete</button>`;
        
        // Capture the brand new delete button inside this specific element
        const deleteBtn = newElement.querySelector(".delete-btn");
        
        // Attach the self-destruction sequence
        deleteBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
            newElement.remove();
        });
        
        // Append it to the page
        taskList.appendChild(newElement);
        
        // Clear input field
        taskInput.value = "";
    });
</script>
```

Remove that single line from your file, refresh your browser, and watch your application work perfectly!ä=
