Ójust tell me the error in this         let promoText = document.querySelector("#promo-text");
        let minBtn = document.querySelector("#minimize-btn");
        minBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
            let grabText = promoText.textContent();
            let smallCase = grabText.toLowerCase();
            promoText.textContent = smallCase;
        })ŒYou are so close! You have the logic perfectly laid out, but you hit one tiny syntax speedbump.

### üö® The Error
```javascript
let grabText = promoText.textContent(); // <-- ERROR IS HERE!
```

### üí° Why it broke:
In JavaScript, `.textContent` is a **property** (a variable attached to an element), not a function/method. Because it is a property, you do not use parentheses `()` to read it. 

When you add `()`, JavaScript thinks you are trying to execute a function, which throws a `TypeError: promoText.textContent is not a function`.

---

### üõ†Ô∏è The Easy Fix
Simply remove the parentheses `()` when you read the text! Here is your corrected code block:

```javascript
let promoText = document.querySelector("#promo-text");
let minBtn = document.querySelector("#minimize-btn");

minBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    let grabText = promoText.textContent; // <-- Fixed! Removed ()
    let smallCase = grabText.toLowerCase();
    promoText.textContent = smallCase;
});
```

Fix that single line in your sandbox file, and Problem 2 is officially crushed! Ready to tackle the other 9?±<
