Write your clean ES6+ solutions inside the `<script>` tag of the file above.

#### üé¨ Feature 1: The API Library Grid Mapper
* **Goal:** Target `#render-library-btn` and `#video-grid`. 
* **Logic:** When clicked, use `.map()` to loop through the `apiVideoPayload` array. For each video object, use **Object Destructuring** to unpack `title`, `type`, and `views` in a single line. Return a template literal string representing a layout card structured like this:
  ```javascript
  `<div class="video-card">
      <span class="badge">${type}</span>
      <h4 style="margin:5px 0;">${title}</h4>
      <small style="color:#9ca3af;">Views: ${views}</small>
   </div>`
  ```
* Finally, convert the returned array into a continuous string using `.join("")` and assign it directly to the video grid container's `.innerHTML`.

#### ‚ûï Feature 2: Watchlist Builder with Self-Destruction
* **Goal:** Target `#media-title-input`, `#media-type-select`, `#add-watchlist-btn`, and `#watchlist-container`.
* **Logic:** When the add button is clicked:
  1. Read the text input and choice dropdown values. If the title input is blank, `return` early.
  2. Create a new `<li>` element using `document.createElement()`. Add the class `.watchlist-item` to it using `.classList.add()`.
  3. Set its `.innerHTML` using backticks to display the information nicely alongside a functional delete button:
     ```javascript
     `<span>üé¨ [${typeValue}] ${titleValue}</span>
      <button class="remove-btn">Remove</button>`
     ```
  4. Query the `.remove-btn` strictly inside that newly created element and add a click handler directly to it that triggers its own `.remove()`.
  5. Append the entire item to `#watchlist-container` and clear out the text input field.

#### üîç Feature 3: Industrial Tier Scanner Loops
* **Goal:** Target `#scan-users-btn` and *all* span nodes matching `.tier-tag` via `querySelectorAll`.
* **Logic:** When the scan button is clicked, write a `.forEach()` loop to read each status badge. Inside the loop, run an evaluation condition check: if its `.textContent` exactly matches `"Premium"`, apply the `.premium-user` class name to it using `.classList.add()`. This will automatically color coordinate your premium users amber!

