ƒyeah now i guess i am clear can you tell me something else that i have been wanting to ask lets say i want to do all my elements in figma lets say how would i be doing it just do the js by myself œThis is an incredibly common workflow in modern tech companies! Designers create the visual look in **Figma**, and developers take those designs and bring them to life with **HTML, CSS, and JavaScript**. 

You don't put JavaScript *inside* Figma. Instead, you use Figma as your visual blueprint, and you build the elements yourself in your code editor. 

Here is exactly how a professional developer takes a Figma design and connects their own custom JavaScript to it step-by-step:

---

## üõ†Ô∏è Step 1: Inspect the Figma Design for Layout & CSS
When a designer gives you a Figma file, you don't just look at it‚Äîyou inspect it to grab the sizing and colors.

1. Click on an element in Figma (like a button or a card).
2. Look at the **Inspect/Properties Panel** on the right side of the screen.
3. Figma will show you the exact dimensions, padding, background colors, and border-radius.
4. Copy those styles and paste them right into your **CSS file** under a class name (e.g., `.figma-card` or `.figma-btn`).

---

## üèóÔ∏è Step 2: Build the Naked Structure in HTML
Look at your Figma design and break it down into boxes. Write the HTML structure to match what you see visually.

```html
<div id="product-card" class="figma-card">
    <h3 id="product-title">Figma Item Name</h3>
    <button id="buy-btn" class="figma-btn">Buy Now</button>
</div>
```

---

## ‚ö° Step 3: Write the JavaScript to Make it Alive
Now that your HTML and CSS look identical to the Figma design, you jump into your JavaScript file to add the logic completely by yourself. You target the elements you built from the Figma blueprint and make them interactive.

```javascript
// 1. Target the elements you built from the Figma design
const buyBtn = document.querySelector("#buy-btn");
const productTitle = document.querySelector("#product-title");

// 2. Add your custom JavaScript functionality
buyBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    // Change the text using your handy textContent property!
    productTitle.textContent = "Added to Cart! üõí";
    buyBtn.style.backgroundColor = "#10b981"; // Turn it green!
});
```

---

## üèéÔ∏è What about Dynamic Elements (like our Task Logger)?
If your Figma design shows a list item that is supposed to appear *only* when a button is clicked, you do exactly what we practiced:

1. Look at the design of that list item in Figma.
2. Write the CSS for it (e.g., `.watchlist-item`).
3. In your JavaScript, use `document.createElement("li")` and `classList.add("watchlist-item")` to generate that exact Figma design out of thin air when the user interacts with the page!

At your job tomorrow, you will likely be handed a Figma link, and your main task will be writing the HTML/CSS to make it look right, and then writing the JavaScript to make it actually work. 

