≤<p>IDs to scan: <b style="color: #e2e8f0;">101, 405, 502, 203, 610</b></p> well tell me what this line says it got no id how to then select this and loop through the information ÓThat is a stellar catch! This hits right at the core of how you design data workflows as a software engineer.

### üö® The Short Answer
You **don't** need to select that `<p>` tag or pull the text out of the HTML to run your loop. 

In a real-world application, the data comes from your JavaScript memory (like an array holding database records or API responses), not from reading the raw text written on the screen. That `<p>` tag is just there for the human user to look at! 

---

### üí° How Your JavaScript Logic Handles It

When the user clicks the button, your function already knows what the IDs are because you declare them right inside your code as a hardcoded variable array. 

Here is exactly how you handle that second problem inside your `<script>` tag:

```javascript
// 1. Target the elements that DO have IDs or tags we need to interact with
const scanBtn = document.querySelector("#scan-btn");
const counterOutput = document.querySelector("#counter-output");

// 2. Listen for the user's click action
scanBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    
    // 3. Set up your logical counter variable
    let highClearanceCount = 0;
    
    // 4. Create your data source array directly in JavaScript!
    const employeeIds = [101, 405, 502, 203, 610];
    
    // 5. Run your loop directly on this JavaScript array (not the HTML text)
    employeeIds.forEach((id) => {
        if (id > 400) {
            highClearanceCount++; // Add 1 to the counter
        }
    });
    
    // 6. Write your final calculated answer out to the page!
    counterOutput.textContent = highClearanceCount;
});
```

---

### üß† The Core Pattern to Remember
* **HTML/DOM elements** (`#scan-btn`, `#counter-output`) are your **Inputs** and **Outputs** (how you receive actions and display results).
* **JavaScript Variables & Arrays** (`employeeIds`) are your **Internal Brain Data** where the actual loop calculations happen.

