Take your newly mapped array, stringify it using .join(""), and drop it directly into the .innerHTML of #status-list.¬†i dont get can you give me an example ¥You've got it. Let's lift the hood on exactly how `.map()` and `.join("")` team up to build HTML list items. It‚Äôs like an automated factory assembly line.

---

### üè≠ The Assembly Line Concept
Imagine an array of raw numbers. `.map()` takes those numbers, runs each one through your conditional `if / else` test, and transforms them into an array of HTML text strings. Then `.join("")` glues them into one massive string block that `.innerHTML` can read.



---

### üìù Step-by-Step Code Example

Let's say we have an array of three numbers: `[20, 45, 15]`. Here is exactly what the code looks like:

```javascript
const temperatures = [20, 45, 15];

// Step 1: Use .map() to transform numbers into HTML string items
const mappedArray = temperatures.map((temp) => {
    if (temp > 35) {
        return "<li>‚ö†Ô∏è Danger</li>";
    } else {
        return "<li>‚úÖ Safe</li>";
    }
});

// üîç If you logged 'mappedArray' to the console right now, it would look like this:
// ["<li>‚úÖ Safe</li>", "<li>‚ö†Ô∏è Danger</li>", "<li>‚úÖ Safe</li>"]


// Step 2: Use .join("") to remove the commas and glue them together
const finalHtmlString = mappedArray.join("");

// üîç 'finalHtmlString' looks like this:
// "<li>‚úÖ Safe</li><li>‚ö†Ô∏è Danger</li><li>‚úÖ Safe</li>"


// Step 3: Shove that final string straight into your HTML container!
const statusList = document.querySelector("#status-list");
statusList.innerHTML = finalHtmlString;
```

---

### üí° Why we need `.join("")`
If you forget to use `.join("")` and try to throw your raw `mappedArray` directly into `.innerHTML`, JavaScript will try to print the array's commas onto the screen. Your website will literally display a bunch of ugly commas between your list items! `.join("")` smashes the strings together into one continuous block of text so the layout looks flawless.

