There is just **one final scope bug** left that will cause a crash when you click the button.

### üö® The Final Bug: Variable Scope (`div` is trapped)

Look at where you declared your `let div` variable vs where you are trying to use it:

```javascript
createBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    if (ticketIssue.value === "") return;
    else {
        let div = document.createElement("div"); // üëà Declared INSIDE the 'else' block
        // ... all your setup code ...
    } // üõë The 'else' block ends here! 'div' dies right here.

    ticketQueue.appendChild(div); // üëà CRASH! 'div' does not exist out here.
    ticketIssue.value = "";
})
```

Because you used `let div` *inside* the curly braces `{ ... }` of the `else` statement, that variable only exists within those braces. The moment JavaScript passes that closing brace, `div` is wiped from memory. When the code hits `ticketQueue.appendChild(div);`, it will crash with a `ReferenceError: div is not defined`.

### üõ†Ô∏è The Cleanest Fix
You actually **don't even need the `else` block** at all! Because you have that guard clause (`if (ticketIssue.value === "") return;`), the function stops automatically if the input is empty. Anything below that line *only* runs if there is valid text.

If you delete the `else {` and its matching closing brace, your code layout un-traps the variable perfectly:

```javascript
createBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    if (ticketIssue.value === "") return; // Guard clause stops empty inputs

    // Now everything streams down smoothly in the same scope block!
    let div = document.createElement("div");
    div.classList.add("ticket-item");
    
    if (prioritySelect.value === "URGENT") {
        div.style.borderLeftColor = "#ef4444"; 
    } 
    
    div.innerHTML = `<span><strong>[${prioritySelect.value}]</strong> ${ticketIssue.value}</span> <button class="resolve-btn">Resolve</button>`;
    
    let removeBtn = div.querySelector(".resolve-btn");
    removeBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
        div.remove();
    });
    
    ticketQueue.appendChild(div); // Works perfectly now!
    ticketIssue.value = "";
});
```

Clean that block scoping up, and your Level 3 machine is officially fully operational, bug-free, and production-ready! How does it feel to see the whole system work seamlessly?Ù
