  { guestId: 56, name: "Janis Ian", invitesLeft: 1 }
];
```

### 3. The Requirement
Render each guest into `#guest-list` inside a `div` with the class `"guest-card"`. Each card needs a button labeled `"Check In Link"`. 

Every time the button is clicked, subtract $1$ from that guest's `invitesLeft`. Update the count on the screen instantly. If their `invitesLeft` reaches exactly $0$, change the button text to `"Fully Checked In"` and disable it.

Don't forget to push your cards to the screen! Let's see your clean execution.˜
What do you thinl ‡You are *incredibly* close on this one! You remembered to append the cards to the DOM (`guestList.appendChild(newCard)`), tracked the data state updates perfectly, and correctly isolated the event listener scoping.

However, you have **three small bugs** that will prevent this code from working seamlessly in the browser. Let's trace through them together:

---

### üö® The Friction Points

#### 1. Broken HTML Template and Targeting `span` vs `p`
```javascript
newCard.innerHTML = `<h3>${invitess.name}</h3> <p id="check-in> Invited Person left: <span style="color: blue;"> ${invitess.invitesLeft}</span> </p> 
...
let checkIn = newCard.querySelector("#check-in")
...
checkIn.textContent = "Fully Checked In"
```
* **The Bug:** There are two issues here. First, notice the missing closing quote on your paragraph ID: `id="check-in>`. Second, you targeted the whole paragraph `<p id="check-in">` to update the text. If you run `checkIn.textContent = "Fully Checked In"`, it will erase everything inside the paragraph, including the text "Invited Person left:". 
* **The Fix:** Move the ID (or use a class) directly onto the `<span>` tag wrapping the number. That way, you only change the number or text inside that specific inner layer!

#### 2. Missing Screen Update on the `else` Path
```javascript
} else {
    invitess.invitesLeft -=1
}
```
* **The Bug:** Just like last time, when a guest has `2` invites left, clicking the button runs the `else` block. It successfully subtracts `1` in the JavaScript object's memory, but you didn't write any line here to update the visual text on the screen! The screen will stay stuck showing `2` until they click it a second time.
* **The Fix:** Explicitly update your tracking element's text content inside the `else` block as well.

#### 3. Duplicate Append Child
```javascript
guestList.appendChild(newCard) // First time
let checkIn = newCard.querySelector("#check-in")
let button=newCard.querySelector("#myButton")
guestList.appendChild(newCard) // Second time!
```
* **The Bug:** You called `guestList.appendChild(newCard)` twice inside the loop. While the browser won't crash (it will just move the element to the end of the container), it's redundant code. You only need to call it once!

---

### üõ†Ô∏è The Flawless Compilation

Here is your exact structure with those micro-adjustments cleaned up:

```javascript
let guestList = document.querySelector("#guest-list");

eventGuests.forEach((guest) => {
    let newCard = document.createElement("div");
    newCard.classList.add("guest-card");
    
    // Fixed: Cleaned up the quote typo and put the ID directly on the <span> tag
    newCard.innerHTML = `<h3>${guest.name}</h3> <p> Invited Person left: <span id="invite-count" style="color: blue;">${guest.invitesLeft}</span> </p> 
    <button id="myButton"> Check-In </button>`;
    
    // Target elements inside the card
    let inviteCountSpan = newCard.querySelector("#invite-count");
    let button = newCard.querySelector("#myButton");
    
    // Append the card exactly ONCE to the container
    guestList.appendChild(newCard);
    
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (guest.invitesLeft === 1) {
            guest.invitesLeft -= 1;
            button.disabled = true;
            button.textContent = "Fully Checked In"; // Updated button text per requirement
            inviteCountSpan.textContent = "0"; // Keep the countdown visual synced
        } else {
            guest.invitesLeft -= 1;
            inviteCountSpan.textContent = guest.invitesLeft; // Fixed: Pushed the updated memory count back to the UI!
        }
    });
});
```

