```
* **The Bug:** This attempts to inject your main list container *inside* the single card. It's backward.
* **The Fix:** It should be `directoryList.appendChild(employeeCard)`.

#### 3. Calling Elements Outside Their Scope
```javascript
if (emploi.name.toLowerCase().includes(lowerCase)) {
    employees()
    name.textContent = emploi.name        // ‚ùå Crash!
    department.textContent = ...          // ‚ùå Crash!
}
```
* **The Bug:** Your variables `name` and `department` were declared inside the `employees` function block. The event listener cannot see them or touch them out here. The browser will throw a `ReferenceError`.
* **The Fix:** Let the `employees` function handle setting the text entirely during its loop.

#### 4. Typo in querySelector
* **The Bug:** You wrote `employeeCard.querySelctor(".department")` (missing the "e" in Selector). This will crash the script instantly.

---

### üõ†Ô∏è The Correct Architecture (Study the Flow)

Here is how you bridge these pieces together cleanly. Look at how the function handles the rendering entirely, and the input listener simply calls it with different subsets of data:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Company Directory</title>
    <style>
        .employee-card { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 16px; margin: 10px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="directory-app">
        <h2>Company Directory</h2>
        <input type="text" id="search-box" placeholder="Search employees by name...">
        <div id="directory-list"></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        const companyEmployees = [
            { id: 1, name: "Alice Smith", department: "Engineering" },
            { id: 2, name: "Bob Jones", department: "Design" },
            { id: 3, name: "Charlie Brown", department: "Engineering" },
            { id: 4, name: "Diana Prince", department: "Management" }
        ];

        let directoryList = document.querySelector("#directory-list");
        let inputSearch = document.querySelector("#search-box");

        // STEP 1: Reusable Rendering Engine
        const renderDirectory = (employeeArray) => {
            // Clear out everything currently on screen first!
            directoryList.innerHTML = "";

            // Loop through whatever array is passed into it
            employeeArray.forEach((employee) => {
                let employeeCard = document.createElement("div");
                employeeCard.classList.add("employee-card");
                
                employeeCard.innerHTML = `
                    <h3 class="name">${employee.name}</h3>
                    <p>Department: <span class="department">${employee.department}</span></p>
                `;
                
                // Append the card to the container
                directoryList.appendChild(employeeCard);
            });
        };

        // STEP 2: Initial Render (Show everyone when page loads)
        renderDirectory(companyEmployees);

        // STEP 3: Live Input Filter Listener
        inputSearch.addEventListener("input", () => {
            let lowerCaseSearch = inputSearch.value.toLowerCase();

            // Filter the master list based on what the user types
            const filteredEmployees = companyEmployees.filter((employee) => {
                return employee.name.toLowerCase().includes(lowerCaseSearch);
            });

            // Hand the newly filtered list back to the engine to re-paint the DOM!
            renderDirectory(filteredEmployees);
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### üéØ What changes?
See how clean the event listener becomes? It just says: *Calculate the search text ‚ûî Filter the array ‚ûî Fire the render machine.*

