ì Nested Event Listener Stacking (Memory Leak) can you tell me how actually should this thing be working and how should i actually solve this issue ˆTo understand how event listeners *should* work and how to fix listener stacking permanently, you have to separate **Data State** from **DOM Rendering**.

Here is how the architecture is supposed to function, followed by the exact step-by-step fix.

---

### The Architecture: Single-Directional Flow

Instead of attaching new listeners whenever a user clicks a button, your app should follow a strict 3-step loop:

1. **One Global Event Listener:** You attach **one** permanent click listener to the parent list (`#task-container`) at the root level of your script when the page loads.
2. **State Update:** When a user clicks a task, that single listener catches the event, finds the corresponding task in your `tasks` array, and flips its `completed` boolean (`true` $\leftrightarrow$ `false`).
3. **Re-render UI:** You clear the DOM (`taskList.innerHTML = ""`) and run a single `render()` function that draws the list based on the updated `tasks` array and `currentFilter`.

```
[ User Clicks Task ] ‚îÄ‚îÄ> [ Update `tasks` Array ] ‚îÄ‚îÄ> [ Run render() ] ‚îÄ‚îÄ> [ DOM Updates ]
```

---

### How to Fix the Code (Step-by-Step)

#### Step 1: Create a Single `render()` Function
Combine your array filtering and DOM insertion into **one** function that takes responsibility for drawing the UI.

```javascript
function render() {
    // 1. Clear the container
    taskList.innerHTML = "";

    // 2. Filter the array based on currentFilter
    const filteredTasks = tasks.filter(task => {
        if (currentFilter === "active") return !task.completed;
        if (currentFilter === "completed") return task.completed;
        return true; // "all"
    });

    // 3. Draw items to the DOM
    filteredTasks.forEach(task => {
        const li = document.createElement("li");
        li.classList.add("task-item");
        if (task.completed) li.classList.add("completed");
        
        // Attach the array ID to the DOM element so we can track it easily
        li.dataset.id = task.id; 
        li.textContent = task.text;
        
        taskList.appendChild(li);
    });
}
```

---

#### Step 2: Use Event Delegation at Root Level
Remove **all** click listeners from inside your filter conditions and loops. Place **one** persistent listener on `taskList` at the very bottom of your script:

```javascript
// Persistent Root Listener (Runs ONCE when page loads)
taskList.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    // Check if the clicked element is a task item
    const clickedEl = event.target.closest(".task-item");
    if (!clickedEl) return;

    // Get the ID we stored in dataset
    const taskId = Number(clickedEl.dataset.id);

    // Update the task state in our array
    const targetTask = tasks.find(t => t.id === taskId);
    if (targetTask) {
        targetTask.completed = !targetTask.completed;
    }

    // Re-render the UI to reflect state changes
    render();
});
```

---

#### Step 3: Simplify Filter Buttons
Your filter buttons now only need to change the `currentFilter` variable and trigger `render()`. No nested listeners required!

```javascript
filterBtn.forEach(btn => {
    btn.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
        // Update active class UI on buttons
        filterBtn.forEach(b => b.classList.remove("active"));
        event.target.classList.add("active");

        // Update state variable and re-render
        currentFilter = event.target.dataset.filter;
        render();
    });
});
```

---

### Why This Completely Eliminates Memory Leaks
* **Zero Accumulation:** Listeners are registered **once** when the script initializes. Clicking filter buttons 100 times will register 0 new listeners.
* **Single Source of Truth:** The DOM is completely replaced on each change based on the `tasks` array state. Your data controls the display, not manual element manipulation.€)
