Ri wnated to ask why on container the click was made and not on the element itself πThis comes down to one of the most fundamental concepts in modern JavaScript DOM architecture: **Event Delegation**.

Here is why attaching the listener to the `.container` parent is far superior to placing listeners directly on individual elements:

---

### 1. Handling Dynamic Content (The Core Reason)
When you render products or cart items dynamically with JavaScript, those HTML elements **do not exist when the page first loads**.

* **The Problem with Individual Listeners:** If you try to run `querySelectorAll('.cart').forEach(...)` when the page loads, it only attaches listeners to elements currently on the DOM. As soon as you clear `cartContainer.innerHTML` or render new items, those new buttons **will not have event listeners attached to them**. They will be completely dead.
* **The Delegation Solution:** By placing **one listener** on a parent container (`.container`) that exists permanently in the HTML, it catches click events from **any** child element inside it‚Äîeven elements created 10 minutes from now!

---

### 2. Performance & Memory Management
* **Direct Listeners:** If you have a catalog of 100 items, and a cart with 50 items, attaching listeners to every single `+`, `-`, and `remove` button means creating **hundreds of active event listeners** in memory.
* **Delegation:** You use **one single listener** in memory for the entire app section.

---

### 3. How It Works Behind the Scenes: Event Bubbling
When a user clicks a button inside your page, JavaScript doesn't *just* trigger the event on that button. The click event "bubbles up" through the DOM tree like a rising bubble in water:

$$\text{Click Target } (<button>) \longrightarrow \text{Parent } (<div>) \longrightarrow \text{Container } (.container) \longrightarrow \text{Document}$$

Because the event travels up to `.container` anyway, you can intercept it there and inspect `e.target` to ask: *"Hey, did this click originate from a `.cart` button?"*

```javascript
document.querySelector(".container").addEventListener("click", (e) => {
    // e.target is the exact element that was clicked
    const cartBtn = e.target.closest(".cart");
    
    if (cartBtn) {
        // Run code specifically for the cart button
    }
});
```

This pattern keeps your DOM clean, prevents memory leaks, and guarantees that dynamically added elements always work seamlessly!∏
