### Step 1: Replace the Hardcoded Array with `fetch`

Delete your static `storeProducts` array and create an `async` function to get data from the API:

```javascript
// State to hold fetched products
let storeProducts = [];

// Fetch products from API
const fetchProducts = async () => {
    try {
        const response = await fetch('https://dummyjson.com/products?limit=8');
        const data = await response.json();
        
        // Map API data to fit your existing structure
        storeProducts = data.products.map(product => ({
            id: product.id,
            name: product.title,
            price: product.price
        }));

        // Render products to screen after fetching
        visualisation(storeProducts);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Failed to fetch products:", error);
    }
};
```

---

### Step 2: Initialize Everything at Startup

Update the bottom of your script so that loading the saved cart and fetching the products happen cleanly when the page loads:

```javascript
// 1. Hydrate Cart & Theme from localStorage
const savedCart = localStorage.getItem("cartItems");
carte = savedCart ? JSON.parse(savedCart) : [];
saveAndRender();

// 2. Fetch Live Products from API
fetchProducts();
```

---

### Why this is a huge step forward:
1. **Asynchronous Flow:** Your code now waits for data from an external network before trying to render products.
2. **Dynamic UI:** You are decoupling the UI logic from static local data‚Äîyour cart and render functions automatically adapt to whatever the API sends back.

