### Step 2: Build the `async` Fetcher Function

Add this function right above your `visualisation()` function. Read through the comments to see how `async`, `await`, and `fetch()` work together:

```javascript
// 1. "async" marks this function as one that will handle background tasks
const getProductsFromAPI = async () => {
    try {
        // 2. "await" pauses THIS function until the network request finishes
        const response = await fetch('https://dummyjson.com/products?limit=8');
        
        // 3. Convert the incoming raw stream into usable JSON format
        const data = await response.json();
        
        // 4. Map the API response to match the exact keys your UI expects (id, name, price)
        storeProducts = data.products.map(item => ({
            id: item.id,
            name: item.title,
            price: item.price
        }));

        // 5. Render the products now that we actually have them!
        visualisation(storeProducts);

    } catch (error) {
        // If the internet drops or the URL is bad, catch the error cleanly
        console.error("Oops, could not load products:", error);
    }
};
```

---

### Step 3: Wire it up at Startup

Scroll down to the **very bottom** of your script. Update your initialization block so it loads your saved cart state *and* kicks off the API request:

```javascript
// AT THE VERY BOTTOM OF YOUR SCRIPT:

// 1. Hydrate the local cart state from localStorage
const savedCart = localStorage.getItem("cartItems");
carte = savedCart ? JSON.parse(savedCart) : [];
saveAndRender();

// 2. Trigger the asynchronous API call to get store items
getProductsFromAPI();
```

