Here is why this sequence will save you weeks of frustration and make you a far better developer:

---

## Why You Shouldn't Jump to React *Today*

If you jump into React right now, you‚Äôll be fighting **two battles at once**:
1. Learning React‚Äôs unique rules (components, JSX, `useState`, `useEffect`, rendering lifecycles).
2. Trying to master core JS concepts (like object destructuring, modern array methods, and async state) at the same time.

When something breaks in React‚Äîand it will!‚Äîyou won't know if it's a bug in your JavaScript logic or a misuse of React.

---

## Why 1‚Äì2 Vanilla JS Projects First Changes Everything

When you build another real project using pure JS (for example, a **Movie Search App** or a **Dashboard with filtering & API loading states**), two magic things happen:

1. **You encounter "The Pain Point":** You will realize how tedious it is to manually rewrite `.innerHTML`, update attributes, and keep the DOM synced with your data arrays. 
2. **React becomes an instantly obvious solution:** When you finally open React, you won't be memorizing arbitrary rules‚Äîyou'll say, *"Ah! React exists specifically to solve that annoying DOM manual-rendering problem I dealt with last week!"*

---

